Subject: SPE Java migration — root cause found, resolved on Java 11 (recommendation + plan)

Hi team,

Summary up front: the out-of-memory failures blocking the SPE Java migration are root-caused and resolved. It is a Java-21-specific incompatibility with Jython; running the SPE runtime on Java 11 (a Jython-supported version) eliminates it, with no application or framework code change. Staging has been stable on Java 11.

BACKGROUND
After moving SPE to Java 21 (Jython upgraded 2.7.0 -> 2.7.4), instances ran out of metaspace within minutes of processing tasks and had to be restarted. We saw the same on an earlier Java 21 attempt that was rolled back.

ROOT CAUSE
Jython compiles each Python script into Java classes at runtime. On Java 8 and 11 it reuses those generated classes across tasks, so memory stays flat. On Java 21, the JDK module system (strong encapsulation) blocks the reflective access Jython uses to define classes into a shared, reusable classloader. Jython falls back to a path that still works but creates a fresh classloader per task, so the generated classes accumulate in metaspace and are never unloaded (they are pinned by live Jython type objects). The result is a steady metaspace leak ending in OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace.

This is not our code. The same scripts and framework run cleanly on Java 8/11. It is a platform-level incompatibility. The Jython project officially supports/tests 2.7.4 only on Java 8 and 11 (references below), and explicitly notes that running under the module system needs extra java command-line options.

EVIDENCE
- GC logs: back-to-back Full GCs triggered by metaspace, reclaiming essentially nothing.
- Heap dump (Eclipse MAT): ~1.5M Jython PyType objects under a single classloader; growth concentrated in non-class metaspace (generated bytecode + constant pools).
- Prod comparison, same code and workload: metaspace flat at ~150 MB on Java 8 vs ramping to ~10 GB on Java 21.
- Measured leak: ~5 MB of metaspace per task, never reclaimed, even at light load (~70 tasks in 30 minutes). A healthy run adds ~0 per task after warmup.

WHAT WE TRIED
We added JVM --add-opens flags to re-enable the reflective access. This cut the leak ~20x (OOM in minutes -> OOM in hours) but did not eliminate it, because the residual regeneration comes from the framework re-importing each script per task, which we cannot change. There is no blanket workaround on Java 21: the global escape hatch (--illegal-access) was made obsolete in Java 17 (JEP 403), leaving only per-package --add-opens.

RESOLUTION: RUN ON JAVA 11
Pointing the runtime image at Java 11 (eclipse-temurin:11-jre-jammy; our build JDK was already 11) resolves the leak at the root. Per-task metaspace cost drops to ~0 because Jython reuses the classes, exactly as on our current Java 8 production.
- Supported Jython configuration (tested on Java 8 and 11).
- No application or framework code change.
- Still achieves the migration goal: off end-of-life Java 8 onto a current, supported LTS.
- Staging has been stable on Java 11.

JAVA 21 (FOR THE RECORD)
Java 21 becomes viable only if the framework is changed to reuse the Jython interpreter / cache compiled scripts so classes are not regenerated per task, or if we move off Jython (which is Python-2 only and in light maintenance). That is a separate, larger effort and should not block this migration. I will raise the interpreter-reuse point with the framework owners.

SEPARATE ISSUE (NOT VERSION-RELATED)
One staging host also hit a heap (not metaspace) OutOfMemoryError from a single ~1 GB allocation inside a task. This is data-driven and unrelated to the Java version; it is tracked separately. (Heap-dump capture was failing on a path collision, now fixed.)

PROPOSED TIMELINE
- Build + deploy to staging: ~1 day (done/in progress)
- Staging soak across a full business cycle: ~3-5 days, to confirm metaspace stays flat
- Functional + performance sign-off: in parallel, ~2-3 days
- Phased production rollout (canary -> fleet) with metaspace monitoring; 2 GB metaspace cap + fail-fast as a safety net
- Rollback: trivial, revert the runtime image tag
Technical readiness ~1-1.5 weeks assuming a clean soak; final production date depends on the change/approval window.

